- op: discuss  # 7838
  at: '2026-08-26T08:28:15Z'  # 7838
  by: agent  # 7838
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7838
  clock: 4  # 7838
  nonce: '7838'  # 7838
  message: "MECHANISM FIXED, MERGED as PR #545. The disjunction is now expanded on BOTH\nconsumer paths, so a module_completeness entry CAN match a C++ call site. Both\ncallers derive from one `module_hint_disjuncts`, so classification and coverage\ncannot drift about what a disjunct is -- which is the failure this contract\nalready suffered once.\n\nALL, NOT ANY, and the direction is deliberate. Classification asks \"does any\nspelling name a primitive\" (an ANY answer is a positive claim); this gate asks\n\"was the surface this call could have come from enumerated\", where a non-match is\ninformative only if EVERY possible home was enumerated. ANY would let one\nenumerated header vouch for a file that also includes twenty that are not.\n\nThe unknown is now reported PER DISJUNCT. `string,sys/socket.h,ws2tcpip.h` names\nnothing a reader can act on; `sys/socket` does.\n\nSTATUS: NOT satisfied, and the reason is a fact the filing did not record.\n`module_io_is_enumerated` returns False for EVERY module in both the c and cpp\ncatalogues -- verified live on 'sys/socket', 'stdio', 'unistd', 'string' -- because\nc.yaml and cpp.yaml mention `module_completeness` only in a COMMENT and declare\nzero entries. So C++ repos are still withheld, and C repos are equally withheld\nby a different route (single module, still not enumerated), which the filing's\n\"C is CLEAN at 0%\" line does not cover -- that 0% is the comma-joined SHARE, not\ncoverage.\n\nWHAT CHANGED IS THE MODALITY: C++ coverage was IMPOSSIBLE and is now merely UNMET.\nThat is the state WI-lutuh's enumeration campaign can act on, and it is exactly\nwhat this item was filed to unblock. Demonstrated in both directions by overlay\ntest: enumerate all three includes and the call is covered; enumerate two and it\nis still withheld, naming the third.\n\nCLOSE THIS ITEM when a C++ repo reaches a non-withheld boundary verdict on real\nenumeration -- that is behavioral evidence for a behavioral statement, and the\nproxy (\"the expansion works\") is not a substitute."  # 7838
- op: discuss  # aa4f
  at: '2026-08-26T22:40:01Z'  # aa4f
  by: agent  # aa4f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # aa4f
  clock: 5  # aa4f
  nonce: aa4f  # aa4f
  message: "NOT YET SATISFIED -- A THIRD CONSUMER. Re-checked 2026-08-26 on merged dev\n(b0b0a2ee25) before closing this item, because its statement says EVERY consumer\npath and the filed repro only exercises two.\n\nTHE SHIPPED HALF WORKS, verified behaviourally rather than by reading the diff.\nFixture: one cpp file with three system includes (<string>, <sys/socket.h>,\n<ws2tcpip.h>) and an unresolved call, claim net_send must_not_exist.\nArtifacts: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/zimud_check_08262026/\n\n  reason string now: \"3 module(s) ... (string, sys/socket, ws2tcpip)\"\n\nThree SEPARATE per-header names, not the pseudo-module\n`string,sys/socket.h,ws2tcpip.h`. That is exactly this item's distinction --\nPERMANENTLY unexaminable has become MERELY unexamined. The verdict is still\ninconclusive, but now for the correct and DIFFERENT reason: cpp.yaml declares\nzero module_completeness, so those three headers have never been audited. That\nis WI-lutuh's job, not this item's. (EXCLUDED does not mean ZERO: a different\nblocker is firing.)\n\nTHE THIRD CONSUMER, found by enumerating every caller of\nmodule_io_is_enumerated rather than trusting the two the item names:\n\n    io_boundary.py:2533\n        if module_hint and catalog.module_io_is_enumerated(module_hint):\n            continue\n\nThis is the external_potential SUPPRESSION gate (\"the catalog has flagged that\nexact module as completeness: complete ... an unmatched call into the module is\nprovably NOT I/O -- so suppress the chain\"). It hands the RAW slot to\nmodule_io_is_enumerated with no expansion, so for any multi-include C++ file the\nargument is the comma-joined pseudo-module and the test can NEVER be true. The\nsuppression simply never fires for C++.\n\nDIRECTION OF THE DEFECT, stated because it changes the urgency and not the\ncorrectness: this one is FAIL-SAFE. Not suppressing means MORE external_potential\nchains are disclosed, never fewer -- the opposite of a false all-clear. And it\nis INERT TODAY, because cpp.yaml has zero module_completeness entries, so the\ngate would not fire even if the string matched.\n\nWHY IT STILL HAS TO BE FIXED NOW: it goes live the moment WI-lutuh gives cpp\nmodule_completeness entries, which is the very next stage of the owner's arc. A\nlatent wrong answer that activates exactly when the adjacent work lands is worse\nthan one that never activates.\n\nTHE QUANTIFIER MUST BE ALL, NOT ANY, matching the coverage gate's reasoning.\nSuppression is a POSITIVE claim that the call is not I/O, so it is only sound\nwhen EVERY disjunct is enumerated; one unenumerated header means the call could\nhave come from a surface nobody audited. ANY would let a single audited header\nvouch for a file that also includes twenty unaudited ones -- the fail-open\ndirection."  # aa4f
